1
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:47,439
The fast flux test facility, a test reactor operating at high core temperatures.

2
00:00:47,439 --> 00:00:51,679
At the FFTF, sodium is used as a coolant to carry reactor heat.

3
00:00:51,679 --> 00:00:55,079
There are several reasons sodium has been selected.

4
00:00:55,079 --> 00:00:58,760
One is that it doesn't slow down the fast neutrons in the fission process as much as

5
00:00:58,759 --> 00:01:05,120
in water-cooled reactors. Another is that sodium has a high boiling point, enabling it to be used

6
00:01:05,120 --> 00:01:10,819
at essentially atmospheric pressure. In the event of a leak then, the sodium would not be driven out

7
00:01:10,819 --> 00:01:16,239
as it would in a pressurized reactor. The high temperature at which sodium can be operated will

8
00:01:16,239 --> 00:01:22,379
result in efficient electricity generation in future liquid metal-cooled reactors. Additionally,

9
00:01:22,879 --> 00:01:27,539
sodium has excellent heat transfer properties, which would allow the use of a more compact

10
00:01:27,540 --> 00:01:32,740
reactor core. As you know, there are some potential hazards associated with sodium.

11
00:01:33,840 --> 00:01:38,140
Your awareness of these hazards is an important part of the assurance that there will be no

12
00:01:38,140 --> 00:01:45,180
incidents. One concern is the possibility of radioactive contamination. Contamination and

13
00:01:45,180 --> 00:01:49,500
activation of the sodium can result from the high neutron density within the reactor itself.

14
00:01:50,460 --> 00:01:54,820
Because of this condition in the reactor, the contaminated sodium will be limited to the

15
00:01:54,820 --> 00:02:00,240
primary loop, only the sodium's heat will be transferred. This is done by an intermediate

16
00:02:00,240 --> 00:02:05,880
heat exchanger to a secondary loop of sodium which is not radioactive. After passing through

17
00:02:05,880 --> 00:02:10,379
the secondary loop, the heat will be released to the atmosphere through these heat exchangers.

18
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:19,680
Sodium was in solid form when it arrived in tank cars at FFTF. In this form, it was not

19
00:02:19,680 --> 00:02:25,280
radioactive. Later it was melted in the tank cars through heating, then transferred to the loops.

20
00:02:27,120 --> 00:02:31,680
Warning signs and barriers are placed to indicate the location of sodium in the FFTF.

21
00:02:32,480 --> 00:02:38,719
Be sure to observe and obey these signs. And before you enter any area marked as restricted,

22
00:02:38,719 --> 00:02:44,480
be sure you are authorized to enter. If you are in doubt, check with the control room first.

23
00:02:44,479 --> 00:02:51,479
Normal operating temperatures in the FFTF range between 400 and 1000 degrees Fahrenheit.

24
00:02:51,479 --> 00:02:57,479
Since sodium can spontaneously ignite in air at temperatures as low as 238 degrees Fahrenheit,

25
00:02:57,479 --> 00:03:02,479
we have designed several features into the plant to moderate the effects of a sodium fire.

26
00:03:02,479 --> 00:03:09,479
For example, pressure in the system itself is so low, sodium leaks would usually be very small.

27
00:03:09,479 --> 00:03:16,299
small. Primary sodium systems will be in steel-lined cells which are inerted with nitrogen. Because

28
00:03:16,299 --> 00:03:22,500
it normally won't contact an oxygen atmosphere, any leakage of primary sodium would not ignite.

29
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:28,939
It would flow to the floor and solidify, where it could be easily cleaned up. Secondary sodium

30
00:03:28,939 --> 00:03:33,939
systems will be in cells which can be flooded with nitrogen to extinguish sodium fires.

31
00:03:39,480 --> 00:03:47,520
Here at a test site near the 300 area, 20 pounds of sodium is heated to its ignition point.

32
00:03:50,780 --> 00:03:55,460
The resulting fire is relatively low-lying and gives off little adjacent heat.

33
00:04:00,060 --> 00:04:06,240
A gasoline fire, by comparison, burns much hotter, sending flames several feet into the air.

34
00:04:06,240 --> 00:04:12,780
Although a sodium fire appears tame, it cannot be extinguished in the usual way.

35
00:04:13,420 --> 00:04:14,040
Here's why.

36
00:04:14,480 --> 00:04:18,879
When sodium combines with water, it forms sodium hydroxide and hydrogen.

37
00:04:19,420 --> 00:04:22,160
The hydrogen is ignited by the heat of the burning sodium.

38
00:04:23,160 --> 00:04:26,680
So, rather than extinguishing it, water would feed the flames,

39
00:04:26,980 --> 00:04:31,780
causing a more serious fire, and under certain conditions, explosions can result.

40
00:04:36,240 --> 00:04:42,560
Water, then, cannot be used to fight a sodium fire.

41
00:04:42,560 --> 00:04:48,639
Carbon dioxide extinguishers are also ineffective because if dry ice, which normally forms on

42
00:04:48,639 --> 00:04:54,920
the nozzle, drops in the sodium, an explosion results.

43
00:04:54,920 --> 00:05:01,960
Therefore, neither water-filled nor CO2 extinguishers should be found near a sodium system.

44
00:05:01,959 --> 00:05:08,459
liquid metal fires at FFTF will be fought with nitrogen or NACs. NACs is a fluidized

45
00:05:08,459 --> 00:05:14,659
form of sodium carbonate. This compound will not interact with sodium, so it is an effective

46
00:05:14,659 --> 00:05:21,659
extinguishing for sodium fires. NACs extinguishers have a star marking and are yellow. They are

47
00:05:21,979 --> 00:05:28,979
also labeled NACs. When NACs is sprayed over a sodium fire, it breaks the fire's chain

48
00:05:28,980 --> 00:05:34,960
It breaks the fire's chain reaction by removing one of the vital links, oxygen.

49
00:05:34,960 --> 00:05:44,540
As you see, a small sodium fire is easily extinguished by coating the surface with NACs.

50
00:05:44,540 --> 00:05:53,360
After the sodium is allowed to cool, it solidifies and can then be removed.

51
00:05:53,360 --> 00:05:57,439
This special gear is an added protection for personnel working in an area where there is

52
00:05:57,439 --> 00:06:05,319
a sodium leak potential. It includes protective clothing, a hard hat with face shield, and

53
00:06:05,319 --> 00:06:12,000
spats to protect the shoes. Although the risk of a sodium leak or fire is minimal, there

54
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:16,920
is still the potential that such an incident could occur. With your help, however, we can

55
00:06:16,920 --> 00:06:22,259
be prepared for such an incident and reduce the consequences. In the event of a sodium

56
00:06:22,259 --> 00:06:27,339
fire, you should inform the control room and other personnel so that the area can be evacuated

57
00:06:27,340 --> 00:06:33,340
immediately. Operations and maintenance personnel at FFTF are trained to respond in emergency

58
00:06:33,340 --> 00:06:39,300
situations and fight any fire until the fire department arrives.

59
00:06:39,300 --> 00:06:44,680
Another factor to consider is personal contact with sodium. Liquid sodium contact with your

60
00:06:44,680 --> 00:06:51,680
skin poses two hazards. First, since your body is largely water, the sodium will interact

61
00:06:51,680 --> 00:06:56,780
resulting in a caustic or chemical burn.

62
00:06:56,780 --> 00:07:02,340
Second, because most sodium in FFTF will be above 300 degrees Fahrenheit, you could receive

63
00:07:02,340 --> 00:07:08,340
a high temperature burn similar to the kind you would get from hot solder.

64
00:07:08,340 --> 00:07:13,280
If liquid sodium comes into contact with your skin, you can reduce injury by following these

65
00:07:13,280 --> 00:07:14,780
simple steps.

66
00:07:14,780 --> 00:07:17,040
Brush the sodium off.

67
00:07:17,040 --> 00:07:21,639
Immediately remove all clothing and get under a safety shower.

68
00:07:21,639 --> 00:07:27,159
stay under the shower for at least 15 minutes. This will allow the remaining sodium to be

69
00:07:27,159 --> 00:07:33,199
fully reacted with the shower water rather than with your skin. The reason you must remove

70
00:07:33,199 --> 00:07:37,800
all clothing prior to entering the safety shower is to prevent any sodium which might

71
00:07:37,800 --> 00:07:42,079
be trapped in the folds of the clothing or in the pockets from interacting with the water

72
00:07:42,079 --> 00:07:48,519
in the shower. As you saw earlier the interaction of sodium with water could cause an explosion

73
00:07:48,519 --> 00:07:50,659
leading to further injury.

74
00:07:50,659 --> 00:07:55,219
In a sodium fire the white smoke produced is made up of sodium oxide.

75
00:07:55,219 --> 00:07:58,019
It is very acrid and unpleasant.

76
00:07:58,019 --> 00:08:02,819
But more important, this smoke can interact with the moisture in your lungs,

77
00:08:02,819 --> 00:08:07,699
your eyes, or even with perspiration on your skin.

78
00:08:07,699 --> 00:08:11,599
This interaction produces sodium hydroxide and again

79
00:08:11,599 --> 00:08:14,759
this can result in a caustic burn.

80
00:08:14,759 --> 00:08:16,659
If a sodium fire occurs,

81
00:08:16,660 --> 00:08:21,100
Avoid contact or inhalation of the smoke.

82
00:08:21,100 --> 00:08:25,520
As we have shown any contact with sodium, no matter how minor,

83
00:08:25,520 --> 00:08:28,660
can produce caustic burns.

84
00:08:28,660 --> 00:08:32,860
After washing the sodium off, go to first aid immediately.

85
00:08:32,860 --> 00:08:37,399
Do not ignore the sodium contact because the sodium hydroxide burn will continue

86
00:08:37,399 --> 00:08:38,740
to grow.

87
00:08:38,740 --> 00:08:43,240
This effect can easily be counteracted by treatment at first aid.

88
00:08:43,240 --> 00:08:46,460
We have had extensive experience in handling sodium.

89
00:08:46,460 --> 00:08:50,660
at the high temperature sodium facility in the 300 area,

90
00:08:50,660 --> 00:08:55,700
in the 200 area, and at EBR 2 in Idaho Falls.

91
00:08:55,700 --> 00:09:00,740
We also have information on dealing effectively with sodium fires.

92
00:09:00,740 --> 00:09:04,660
We're pleased to report there have been no major leaks or serious injuries.

93
00:09:04,660 --> 00:09:08,379
This is a record we need your help in maintaining.

94
00:09:08,379 --> 00:09:11,980
The actions you take in the event of a sodium leak or fire are simple and

95
00:09:11,980 --> 00:09:13,620
effective.

96
00:09:13,620 --> 00:09:18,720
With your help We will all continue to work in a safe and healthy environment at FFTF.
